config TORABO_RESERVED_LAYERS
	int "Number of reserved (runtime-claimable) keymap layers, as advertised to the app"
	default 0
	range 0 10
	help
	  How many empty status="reserved" layers this build has, so torabo-caps can
	  advertise the count to the app. 0 = none.

	  ⚠ This does NOT create the layers. Zephyr processes devicetree before
	  Kconfig and does not pass autoconf.h to the DTS preprocessor, so CONFIG_*
	  is always undefined inside an overlay. The torabo-reserved-layers snippet
	  therefore takes its count from the TORABO_RESERVED_LAYERS *preprocessor*
	  macro (default 10), set from build.yaml:

	      cmake-args: -DDTS_EXTRA_CPPFLAGS="-DTORABO_RESERVED_LAYERS=6"

	  Keep this Kconfig value equal to that number (the firmware-builder emits
	  both together).

	  ⚠ C-5 (docs/COMPATIBILITY.md, PLAN-ext-fw-refactor.md phase 7): this value
	  and the DTS_EXTRA_CPPFLAGS macro are two independent numbers with no build-time
	  check that they agree. If they drift apart, torabo_caps (features/caps/src/caps.c,
	  build_features()'s TORABO_FEAT_RESERVED_LAYERS entry) advertises THIS
	  Kconfig value to the app as the reserved-layer count, while the devicetree
	  actually injected however many layers the DTS_EXTRA_CPPFLAGS macro said —
	  a silent lie the app cannot detect (it has no independent way to see the
	  real layer count). Getting them wrong does not break the build or the
	  firmware's own behavior; it only misleads the app's layer picker. Always
	  change both numbers together.

	  Assumes the torabo-tsuki 66-key layout (rows 12/12/14/14/14). Do NOT also
	  declare reserved layers in your keymap.keymap when using this snippet (the
	  counts would stack).
